<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zalavár</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mosapurc, Mosaburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zalavár is a village in Zala county in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, near the south-western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-balaton">Lake Balaton</a>. In the 9th century, as Mosapurc or Mosaburg, it was the fortified seat of the Slavic princes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocel">Kocel</a> under Frankish overlordship, built on an island in the marshes of the Zala river. Excavations have revealed the remains of several churches, including a large church consecrated by the archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zalavar.jpg" alt="Zalavár" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zalav%C3%A1r_-_Convent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
